debugger-fatal-exit-live-cpu: a fatal error under the debugger ends MAME with its own exit code
version: 1
base: 9c28def3407cf3386d5c6bea932312741acb7992

This is the fleet's canonical copy of a change to MAME's debugger, proposed upstream as
mamedev/mame#16363. A MAME repository vendors it as patches/0005-debugger-fatal-exit-live-cpu.patch
and applies it after its own patches.

What it fixes: `debugger_cpu::start_hook` makes a cpu the live one before it executes and
`stop_hook` clears it afterwards, with nothing to clear it if an exception passes in between. A
fatal error thrown while a cpu executes (a cpu core's own `fatalerror()`, or a debugger hook such
as a gdbstub without 0004 refusing a cpu it has no description of) therefore leaves that cpu live
while `running_machine::run` tears the machine down. `device_t::stop` resets each device's
`device_debug`, and `std::unique_ptr::reset` nulls the pointer before it runs the destructor.
`~device_debug` clears its breakpoints and registerpoints, and both end in
`breakpoint_update_flags`, which calls `live_cpu()->debug()->compute_debug_flags()`. From the
moment the live cpu's own `device_debug` is destroyed, that `debug()` is null, for the live cpu
and for every device stopped after it, and MAME segfaults instead of exiting with its fatal
error's code.

Why it is safe: a cpu becomes the live one through its own `debug()` (`debugger_start_cpu_hook`
calls `device().debug()->start_hook()`), so while the machine runs the live cpu always has its
`device_debug` and the added test is true. It is false only in the teardown after such a fatal
error, when no cpu executes again.

diff --git a/src/emu/debug/debugcpu.cpp b/src/emu/debug/debugcpu.cpp
index 7c742fe..c0bbd29 100644
--- a/src/emu/debug/debugcpu.cpp
+++ b/src/emu/debug/debugcpu.cpp
@@ -1967,9 +1967,11 @@ void device_debug::breakpoint_update_flags()
 		}
 	}
 
-	// push the flags out globally
-	if (m_device.machine().debugger().cpu().live_cpu() != nullptr)
-		m_device.machine().debugger().cpu().live_cpu()->debug()->compute_debug_flags();
+	// push the flags out globally; a fatal error thrown while a CPU executes leaves it live,
+	// and once that CPU's device_debug is being destroyed its debug() is null
+	device_t *const livecpu = m_device.machine().debugger().cpu().live_cpu();
+	if (livecpu != nullptr && livecpu->debug() != nullptr)
+		livecpu->debug()->compute_debug_flags();
 }
 
 
